\section{Discussion and Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}

This work identifies a systematic contamination in VLA collision avoidance evaluation: standard CAR conflates genuine obstacle avoidance with inaction caused by action collapse. Across all Pi0-family configurations evaluated on SafeLIBERO, over 98\% of episodes classified as safe exhibit sub-threshold displacement, rendering raw CAR unreliable as a safety metric. Displacement-filtered CAR (CAR$_\text{f}$) separates these two behaviors by excluding episodes below a displacement threshold $\tau_d$, providing a metric that credits only active avoidance. Two structural findings reinforce this diagnosis: TSR and CAR are anti-correlated under current methods (Section~\ref{sec:tsr_car}), and end-effector-only SDF refinement fails to prevent full-body collisions even with ground-truth geometry (Section~\ref{sec:ee_only}). Training-time SDF supervision on Pi0.5 is the sole configuration that produces non-zero CAR$_\text{f}$ with zero frozen rate, placing it in a region of the TSR-CAR plane that no other evaluated method occupies (Section~\ref{sec:a1_pi05}).

\paragraph{Limitations.}
The empirical scope of this study is bounded in several respects. All experiments use the \texttt{object\_pudding} Level~I suite from SafeLIBERO (4 tasks). The fake safety mechanism is model-agnostic in principle: any action-collapsed VLA will receive inflated CAR. Direct verification, however, covers only the Pi0 and Pi0.5 model families; whether the same contamination pattern appears in OpenVLA, RT-2, or other VLA architectures requires additional experiments. The EE-only SDF oracle (Section~\ref{sec:ee_only}) tests end-effector-level refinement exclusively; full-body SDF monitoring along the kinematic chain may yield different outcomes. The displacement threshold $\tau_d = 0.1$\,mm is empirically robust across four orders of magnitude (Appendix~\ref{app:threshold_sensitivity}), but remains task-dependent in principle. Pi0.5+A1 results are reported at the 10K-step checkpoint (seed~0); the 30K checkpoint is pending, and the final magnitude of the TSR-CAR tradeoff is not yet determined.

\paragraph{Future work.}
Four directions follow from this analysis. First, full-body SDF monitoring along the robot kinematic chain would address the structural gap identified in Section~\ref{sec:ee_only}. Second, evaluation across additional SafeLIBERO suites and other manipulation benchmarks would test whether the contamination patterns generalize beyond the task family studied here. Third, cross-architecture evaluation on non-Pi0-family VLAs (OpenVLA, RT-2) would determine the prevalence of fake safety in the broader VLA landscape. Fourth, we recommend that future VLA safety evaluations report both raw CAR and CAR$_\text{f}$; the gap between the two quantifies the degree of action-collapse contamination in a given model.

\paragraph{Broader impact.}
Metric reliability is a prerequisite for safe deployment of autonomous manipulation systems. Contaminated evaluation metrics create a risk that models with no genuine collision avoidance capability pass safety benchmarks and proceed to deployment. Displacement filtering is a low-cost correction: it requires only end-effector position logging, no additional data collection or model modification. The analysis in this work is conducted entirely in simulation; real-world deployment introduces additional safety considerations (sensor noise, sim-to-real transfer, dynamic obstacles) that fall outside the present scope.

\section{Experimental Framework}\label{sec:framework}

This section defines the evaluation platform, metrics, and model configurations that underpin the analyses in Sections~\ref{sec:fake_safety}--\ref{sec:a1_pi05}. Our primary methodological contribution is the displacement-filtered Collision Avoidance Rate (CAR$_\text{f}$), which separates genuine avoidance from action collapse.

\subsection{Benchmark and Evaluation Protocol}\label{sec:benchmark}

We conduct all experiments on SafeLIBERO~\cite{hu2025vlsa}, a collision-aware evaluation extension of the LIBERO simulation platform~\cite{liu2023libero}. SafeLIBERO augments standard manipulation benchmarks with per-timestep collision detection and non-target obstacle annotations, enabling systematic measurement of both task completion and safety.

We select the \textit{object\_pudding} Level~I suite, which comprises four tabletop manipulation tasks. In each task, a target object is surrounded by non-target obstacles that the robot must avoid during grasping and transport. The MuJoCo physics engine provides collision detection: any contact between the robot (end-effector or arm links) and a non-target object registers as a collision event.

Each method is evaluated for 50 episodes per task (200 episodes total). For multi-seed experiments (Pi0.5), we run 3 seeds to produce 600 episodes per configuration. Initial object configurations are fixed across methods to ensure fair comparison.

\subsection{Evaluation Metrics}\label{sec:metrics}

\paragraph{Standard metrics.} We report two conventional metrics. The \emph{Task Success Rate} (TSR) is the fraction of episodes where the manipulation task reaches completion. The \emph{Collision Avoidance Rate} (CAR) is the fraction of episodes in which no collision occurs between the robot and any non-target object.

\paragraph{Displacement-filtered CAR.} Standard CAR conflates two qualitatively distinct behaviors: a robot that plans a trajectory around obstacles, and a robot that has collapsed into inaction. Both register as ``safe,'' yet only the former reflects collision avoidance capability. We propose the displacement-filtered CAR (CAR$_\text{f}$), which excludes episodes exhibiting action collapse from both numerator and denominator.

We define an episode $e$ as \emph{action-collapsed} when its maximum end-effector displacement satisfies $d_{\max}(e) < \tau_d$, where $\tau_d = 0.1$\,mm. The filtered metric is then:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:car_f}
\text{CAR}_\text{f} = \frac{|\{e : \text{safe}(e) \;\land\; d_{\max}(e) \geq \tau_d\}|}{|\{e : d_{\max}(e) \geq \tau_d\}|}
\end{equation}
where $d_{\max}(e)$ denotes the maximum Euclidean displacement of the end-effector across all timesteps in episode $e$. The threshold $\tau_d = 0.1$\,mm corresponds to sub-actuator-resolution motion and is robust to perturbation (Appendix~\ref{app:threshold_sensitivity}).

\paragraph{Auxiliary metrics.} We additionally report the \emph{Frozen Rate}, the fraction of episodes with $d_{\max} < \tau_d$, which quantifies the severity of action collapse for each method. The \emph{Simultaneous Success Rate} (SSR $= $ TSR $\cap$ CAR) measures the fraction of episodes that achieve both task completion and collision avoidance.

\subsection{Models and Training}\label{sec:models}

\paragraph{Base architectures.} We evaluate two VLA models from the Pi0 family~\cite{black2024pi0}. \textbf{Pi0} (${\sim}$3B parameters) uses a PaliGemma vision-language encoder paired with a dedicated action expert that generates continuous actions via flow matching~\cite{lipman2023flow}. \textbf{Pi0.5} extends this architecture to ${\sim}$3.5B parameters with broader pre-training data, and serves as a stronger baseline that exhibits less action collapse.

\subsection{The Fake Safety Problem}\label{sec:fake_safety}

At first glance, the collision avoidance results for SDF-augmented VLA models appear compelling. Our A1-LoRA model (Pi0 fine-tuned with an SDF auxiliary loss) achieves a Collision Avoidance Rate (CAR) of 91.5\% across 800 evaluation episodes on the SafeLIBERO benchmark~\cite{hu2025vlsa}, a figure that would suggest reliable obstacle avoidance. Yet this headline metric conceals a fundamental problem. When we examine the end-effector displacement of the 732 episodes classified as ``safe,'' the picture changes entirely: 541 episodes (73.9\%) exhibit a maximum displacement below 1\,$\mu$m, and another 188 episodes (25.7\%) remain below 0.1\,mm. Only 3 episodes (0.4\%) show displacement above the 0.1\,mm threshold, the minimum we consider consistent with intentional motion. The remaining 99.6\% of safe episodes are artifacts of a robot that never moved.

We define a safe episode as \emph{fake safe} when the maximum end-effector displacement stays below a threshold $\tau_d = 0.1$\,mm. Under this criterion, 729 of the 732 safe episodes produced by A1-LoRA are fake safe. The robot did not avoid obstacles; it avoided motion altogether. The displacement distribution, shown in Figure~\ref{fig:displacement}, clusters overwhelmingly near zero, with no meaningful spread toward the range that would indicate planned trajectories around obstacles. Table~\ref{tab:fake_safety} summarizes the fake safety statistics across all evaluated methods.

\subsection{Capability-Safety Anti-correlation}\label{sec:tsr_car}

The displacement analysis in Section~\ref{sec:fake_safety} reveals a striking pattern at the aggregate level: models with higher task capability systematically report lower collision avoidance rates. Pi0.5 achieves a TSR of 74.7\,$\pm$\,1.5\% but a CAR of only 26.8\,$\pm$\,1.6\%, while Pi0-based methods (LoRA baseline, Full-FT, and A1-LoRA) report TSR $\leq$ 0.5\% alongside CAR values of 82--91.5\%. The anti-correlation is consistent across all three Pi0.5 seeds (TSR 73--76\%, CAR 25--28\%) and across Pi0 variants regardless of whether an SDF loss is applied. We observe that models capable of executing tasks also move the robot into configurations where collisions become possible, while models that fail to act avoid collisions by default.

Figure~\ref{fig:tsr_car_scatter} plots all evaluated configurations in TSR-CAR space. Two clusters emerge. Pi0-family methods occupy the upper-left region: high CAR, near-zero TSR. As established in Section~\ref{sec:fake_safety}, over 99\% of their safe episodes are fake safe, placing this cluster in what we term the \emph{fake safety zone}. Pi0.5, by contrast, sits in the lower-right region: high TSR, low CAR. Its collisions reflect genuine robot motion through the workspace. The two clusters are separated by a gap that no evaluated method occupies, a region that would require both meaningful task execution and reliable obstacle avoidance.
